+\clearpage
+\section{Method benchmarks and a further precision refinement of $C_6(2),C_6(3)$}
+\label{app:C6Methods}
+
+This appendix changes the numerical evaluation, not the field-theoretic
+integrals, graph weights, forest subtraction, or definition
+$\epsilon=2s-d$. It benchmarks several sampling maps, accelerates the
+same finite kernels, and adds two independently seeded production
+campaigns. The analytic power-subgraph compensation of the preceding
+appendices is retained and is not recomputed here. The present numbers
+supersede the precision, not the normalization, of Appendix~W.
+
+\subsection{Result and interpretation of the errors}
+The current estimates are
+\begin{equation}\boxed{\begin{aligned}
+ C_6(2)&=362.3\pm0.7,\\
+ C_6(3)&=33.57\pm0.10.
+\end{aligned}}\label{meth:reported}\end{equation}
+The allowances are four estimated sampling standard errors rounded
+upward, using the same convention as Appendix~W. They are empirical
+numerical allowances, not rigorous enclosures, guaranteed confidence
+intervals, or truncation errors for the physical $\epsilon$ expansion.
+The underlying estimator digits are recorded for reproducibility, not
+as independently certified physical digits.
+\begin{center}\begin{tabular}{@{}crrrr@{}}\toprule
+$d$ & Estimator & Previous sampling SE & Current sampling SE & SE gain\\\midrule
+2 & 362.34891380 & 0.226352 & 0.152644 & 1.48 \\
+3 & 33.56819680 & 0.039354 & 0.024184 & 1.63 \\
+\bottomrule\end{tabular}\end{center}
+The previous production data are retained with weights fixed before the
+new production. Fresh pilot and method-validation samples are excluded.
+The lower-loop previous production is also used to plan new allocations;
+with frozen combination weights and independently randomized fresh
+samples this does not change its expectation. No known $C_3,C_4,C_5$
+is used as a fitted control or imposed as a constraint.
+
+\subsection{The quantity being integrated}
+For each loop order $L$, the sampled vector contains the coupling and
+mass Taylor coefficients through regulator order $6-L$:
+\begin{equation}
+ \boldsymbol\mu_L=(b_{L,0},\ldots,b_{L,6-L},
+ c_{L,0},\ldots,c_{L,6-L}),\qquad L=3,4,5,6.
+ \label{meth:jets}
+\end{equation}
+The one- and two-loop inputs remain exact or independently evaluated
+special-function inputs from the earlier calculation. The physical
+coefficient is evaluated as a finite polynomial
+\begin{equation}
+ C_6=\mathcal F_6(\boldsymbol\mu_3,\boldsymbol\mu_4,
+ \boldsymbol\mu_5,\boldsymbol\mu_6)+\eta.
+ \label{meth:functional}
+\end{equation}
+Here $\mathcal F_6$ solves the regulator-dependent finite-MOM fixed-point
+problem before setting $z=\epsilon$; $\eta$ accounts only for separately
+retained numerical kernels. In particular,
+\begin{equation}
+ \frac{\partial C_6}{\partial(b_{6,0},c_{6,0})}
+ =\frac{(-1,3)}{2187}.
+ \label{meth:lastgradient}
+\end{equation}
+One must not replace the Taylor vectors by their values at $z=0$ and
+then apply the old MS assembly. The field-theoretic derivation of these
+finite vectors and their power-sector compensation is unchanged.
+
+The finite kernel retains the pointwise forest sum
+\begin{equation}
+ K_G^{(d)}(x)=\sum_F(-1)^{|F|}
+ \frac{\sum_{e\in E_R(F)}x_e}{S(x)U_F(x)^{d/2}},
+ \qquad S(x)=\sum_e x_e.
+ \label{meth:forest}
+\end{equation}
+Every numerator and denominator is evaluated on the same integration
+point before accumulating the graph estimate. Tropical importance
+sampling uses the known endpoint geometry of these parametric
+integrals~\cite{BorinskyD2Focus}. More samples cannot in general repair
+a representation which postpones subtraction of divergent terms until
+after separate numerical integrations.
+
+\subsection{A faster evaluator with the same integral}
+Each monomial in a loop-$L$ forest polynomial has exactly $L$ distinct
+edge factors. Associate a bit mask $m$ to a monomial and let $j$ be one
+of its set bits. Precompute a directed acyclic product graph obeying
+\begin{equation}
+ p_0=1,\qquad p_m=x_jp_{m\setminus\{j\}}.
+ \label{meth:dag}
+\end{equation}
+All forest terms share these products. This replaces repeated
+$\exp(\sum_{e\in m}\log x_e)$ evaluations by one exponential per edge
+and a shared set of multiplications. For very small coordinates the
+same common-monomial scaling as the original implementation is used:
+\begin{equation}
+ p_m=\prod_{e\in m}\exp\left(\log x_e-\frac{h}{L}\right)
+ =\exp\left(\sum_{e\in m}\log x_e-h\right).
+ \label{meth:scaled}
+\end{equation}
+The common factor is restored outside the forest sum. The direct
+unscaled path is used only in a range checked against the exponent
+range of extended precision. The original binary128 and 300-decimal
+fallbacks for severe cancellation are retained. The numerical kernels
+are not evaluated at lower precision to obtain the speedup.
+
+An identical-point comparison used 1,095,680 six-loop evaluations for
+each dimension, with identical seeds, digital matrices, allocations,
+and importance distributions:
+\begin{center}\begin{tabular}{@{}crrr@{}}\toprule
+$d$ & Original evaluator (s) & Shared products (s) & Speedup\\\midrule
+2 & 7.88237 & 2.77982 & 2.836\\
+3 & 7.74461 & 2.69407 & 2.875\\\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}\end{center}
+These are elapsed times on the current four-CPU-quota environment, not
+hardware-independent throughput claims. The maximum normalized
+per-graph replicate difference was below $9.0\times10^{-13}$.
+The improvement is in cost per evaluation, not in the variance of an
+individual sample. Independently constructed Kirchhoff cofactor
+integrands supplied 1,038 additional checks, including endpoint
+coordinates down to $10^{-130}$; the largest normalized discrepancy
+was $6.88\times10^{-18}$. Neither comparison is an interval certificate
+for the full integral.
+
+\subsection{Sampling methods tested at matched budgets}
+The baseline is independently randomized Sobol sampling inside each
+fixed graph stratum. Sample counts are powers of two separately in
+each stratum; the uncertainty is estimated between independent
+randomized nets, never from the individual dependent points within a
+net~\cite{PrecisionSobol,PrecisionQMC}. The benchmark target is the
+six-loop influence in \eqref{meth:lastgradient}. Its variance is
+estimated as the sum of the graph-stratum replicate variances, divided
+by the number of replicates. The production analysis instead retains
+the full covariance of the loop vectors.
+
+Each pilot comparison used 16 randomizations and 1,095,680 kernel
+evaluations. Antithetic sampling used half as many points with two
+function evaluations each. The table is normalized to the baseline
+variance at the same evaluation budget; smaller is better.
+\begin{center}\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.59\textwidth}rr@{}}\toprule
+Method & $d=2$ & $d=3$\\\midrule
+Original randomized Sobol & 1.000 & 1.000 \\
+Independent pseudo-random Monte Carlo & 1.899 & 1.499 \\
+Radial coordinates placed first & 1.105 & 1.126 \\
+One-coordinate edge-order encoding & 1.083 & 1.055 \\
+Nominal endpoint rate $0.6p$ & 1.667 & 2.301 \\
+Nominal endpoint rate $0.5p$ & 4.194 & 6.194 \\
+Nominal endpoint rate $1.0p$ & 4.452 & 1.797 \\
+Radial antithetic pairs & 1.584 & 1.629 \\
+\bottomrule\end{tabular}\end{center}
+The pilot differences are empirical and subject to finite-replicate
+uncertainty; they are not theorems about the methods. Coordinate
+reordering and compression of all order choices into one uniform
+coordinate gave no convincing improvement. Independent pseudo-random
+sampling was worse than the retained randomized Sobol baseline.
+
+\paragraph{Why a smoother endpoint map was not the winner.}
+An illustrative endpoint integral is
+\begin{equation}
+ \int_0^1t^{p-1}f(t)\,dt
+ =\frac1\alpha\int_0^1
+ u^{p/\alpha-1}f(u^{1/\alpha})\,du.
+ \label{meth:power}
+\end{equation}
+Reducing $\alpha$ can smooth the endpoint power, but it simultaneously
+changes the normalized sampling distribution in every radial
+coordinate. It can move variance into a narrow central region.
+The actual pilot changed all original tropical rates by factors
+$0.75$ and $0.625$, corresponding to nominal endpoint rates $0.6p$
+and $0.5p$ instead of $0.8p$. Those variants lost to the baseline in
+both dimensions. Smoothness alone is not a sufficient optimization
+criterion for the present finite, signed, high-dimensional kernels.
+
+\paragraph{A pilot-frozen separable importance map.}
+A separate training stream estimates the conditional second moment
+in 32 bins for each radial coordinate. Its square root is smoothed,
+clipped, and blended equally with a uniform density. Inverting this
+positive piecewise-linear cumulative distribution defines $u=T(v)$.
+The exact change of measure is
+\begin{equation}
+ \int_{[0,1]^s}f(u)\,du
+ =\int_{[0,1]^s}f(T(v))
+ \prod_{j=1}^sT_j'(v_j)\,dv.
+ \label{meth:transport}
+\end{equation}
+All Jacobian factors are included in the kernel. This is a small
+VEGAS-inspired separable transport, not an implementation or benchmark
+of the full VEGAS+ algorithm. VEGAS+ additionally uses adaptive
+stratification and has documented advantages for some multi-peak and
+diagonal structures~\cite{MethodVegas}.
+
+Fresh validation used 32 randomizations and 4,382,720 evaluations per
+map. Relative variance per evaluation was
+\begin{center}\begin{tabular}{@{}lrr@{}}\toprule
+Frozen transport & $d=2$ & $d=3$\\\midrule
+None & 1.0000 & 1.0000\\
+Three trained axes & 0.9533 & 0.9871\\
+All eleven trained axes & 0.8985 & 0.9905\\\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}\end{center}
+Thus the all-axis map supplied a modest approximately 10\% variance
+reduction at $d=2$, but no material improvement at $d=3$. Only fresh
+campaign A at $d=2,L=6$ uses this map; its independent campaign B retains
+the original map. Both three-dimensional campaigns use the baseline
+map. No map is adapted using its production samples.
+The mathematical map has endpoints $0,1$. Delivered decimal maps have
+endpoint roundoff below $7.8\times10^{-16}$; a same-point replay with
+endpoints snapped exactly to $0,1$ changed the six-loop mean by
+$-2.95\times10^{-13}$. This is a floating-point consistency check,
+not a bound on all digital-discretization effects.
+
+\paragraph{Conditional stratification and linear controls.}
+Exact stratification of the first edge-order choice was also tested;
+its variance per evaluation relative to matched unstratified controls
+was 1.043 at $d=2$ and 1.062 at $d=3$. It was not adopted.
+Separately, the previous coefficient covariance predicts SE ratios
+0.985 and 0.962 for covariance-optimal linear controls using known
+$C_3,C_4,C_5$. This is a diagnostic within that particular linear span,
+not a universal bound, and those controls were not fitted or applied.
+They cannot directly eliminate six-loop sampling variation, since
+$C_3,C_4,C_5$ contain no six-loop input.
+
+\subsection{Cost-aware graph allocation and production plan}
+Let $a_g/n_g$ model the variance of the target contribution from graph
+$g$, and let $c_g$ denote the measured cost of one sample. Minimizing
+this model at fixed cost gives
+\begin{equation}
+ \min_{n_g>0}\sum_g\frac{a_g}{n_g},\qquad
+ \sum_gc_gn_g=T
+ \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
+ n_g=\lambda\sqrt{\frac{a_g}{c_g}}.
+ \label{meth:allocation}
+\end{equation}
+The derivative of $C_6$, rather than raw beta-function variance,
+defines the pilot target. New six-loop allocations use the independent
+method-validation streams. Lower-loop allocations use the previous
+production. Each count is rounded to a power of two and frozen before
+new integration. This is an MC-variance-model allocation used for
+RQMC, not a claim that the RQMC variance is exactly proportional to
+$1/n_g$ or that the allocation is globally optimal.
+
+Each fresh campaign has 32 independently randomized replicates at
+each loop order. The per-replicate point counts are
+\begin{center}\begin{tabular}{@{}crrrr@{}}\toprule
+$d$ & $L=3$ & $L=4$ & $L=5$ & $L=6$\\\midrule
+2 & 135,168 & 264,192 & 1,158,144 & 4,647,936\\
+3 & 151,552 & 305,152 & 1,189,376 & 4,381,184\\\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}\end{center}
+The primary fresh production comprises 782,893,056 kernel evaluations.
+The additional control experiment below is counted separately.
+Pilot, method-validation, speed-test, and endpoint-replay samples are
+not included. Campaigns A and B use different seeds and 52-bit and
+60-bit digital coordinates, respectively. The source and input hashes
+were frozen before production and rechecked afterward.
+
+The complete fresh determinations, before mixing with old production,
+are
+\begin{center}\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}\toprule
+& $C_6(2)$ & Sampling SE & $C_6(3)$ & Sampling SE\\\midrule
+Fresh A & 362.41502790 & 0.275726 & 33.59417512 & 0.040029 \\
+Fresh B & 362.46528830 & 0.273306 & 33.52750475 & 0.039502 \\
+Fresh only combined & 362.44015668 & 0.196310 & 33.56084035 & 0.028119 \\
+\bottomrule\end{tabular}\end{center}
+The primitive two-dimensional input is common to these displayed
