+\subsection{An additional test: dimension-coupled primitive controls}
+The same Sobol matrices and graph-specific shifts can evaluate an
+auxiliary integral at $d=2$ along with the target at $d=3$. This uses
+common random numbers, not an interpolation or conjectured relation
+between the two physical coefficients. For a primitive block $P$,
+\begin{equation}
+ \widehat C_6^{\rm cv}(3)=\widehat C_6(3)
+ -\beta\left(\widehat P^{\rm paired}(2)-\widehat P^{\rm ref}(2)\right).
+ \label{meth:crosscontrol}
+\end{equation}
+The term in parentheses has expectation zero. The two estimates of
+$P(2)$ are independent, but the paired one is correlated with the
+sampled three-dimensional block. The reference estimate is not exact.
+Its variance must enter once:
+\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
+ V_{\rm cv}&=V_3+\beta^2(V_P+\sigma_{\rm ref}^2)
+ -2\beta\operatorname{Cov}(\widehat C_6(3),\widehat P^{\rm paired}(2)),\\
+ \beta_{\rm optimum}&=
+ \frac{\operatorname{Cov}(\widehat C_6(3),\widehat P^{\rm paired}(2))}
+ {V_P+\sigma_{\rm ref}^2}.
+\end{aligned}\label{meth:noisycontrol}\end{equation}
+The optimum formula assumes the indicated population covariance is
+known; it is not a justification for fitting production coefficients
+without error control.
+
+An independent paired pilot fitted three block coefficients; independent
+validation then found residual-variance ratios 0.234, 0.743 and 0.597
+for the primitive, one-subgraph and two-subgraph blocks. Two dimensions
+require twice as many kernel evaluations, so only the primitive block
+passed the chosen equal-evaluation-cost criterion. Its frozen
+coefficient is
+\begin{equation}
+ \beta=0.09955611941934894,
+ \qquad P^{\rm ref}(2)=P_{10}\text{ of \eqref{meth:retained}}.
+ \label{meth:crossbeta}
+\end{equation}
+The existing three-dimensional sample locations were replayed for
+this auxiliary block in all four campaigns, adding 34,144,256
+two-dimensional evaluations, but no new three-dimensional target
+samples. Identical seeds and digital matrices were verified. An
+independent three-dimensional replay of 894,464 primitive evaluations
+reproduced the selected stored graph-replicate values exactly.
+
+The complete controlled result is
+\begin{equation}
+ \widehat C_6^{\rm cv}(3)=33.5681256836,
+ \qquad\widehat\sigma_{\rm cv}=0.0243339897.
+ \label{meth:cvresult}
+\end{equation}
+Although the primitive block's validation variance fell by a factor
+of about 4.3, the full coefficient did not gain meaningful precision.
+The primitive contribution represents only about 5.8\% of the
+uncontrolled coefficient variance. The reference alone contributes
+$\beta\sigma_{P_{10}}=0.00412205$ to the standard error. The block's
+sampling error decreases from 0.00584436 to 0.00325730, but after
+including that reference uncertainty it is 0.00525370. Finite-replicate
+covariance fluctuations make the full controlled error slightly
+larger than the plain 0.02418404. This tiny difference is not evidence
+of a general disadvantage of controls. The original fixed-weight
+plain estimator remains the reported result; the controlled value is
+retained as an additional consistency check and method benchmark.
+
+This example distinguishes optimization of a visually attractive
+subintegral from optimization of the uncertainty on the complete
+physical coefficient. It also shows why an uncertain auxiliary value
+cannot be treated as an exact control mean. No production samples
+were used to fit $\beta$ or to choose the primitive control block.
+
+\subsection{Retained two-dimensional inputs and covariance}
+The preceding calculation replaced 76 six-loop finite kernels by three
+independent numerical groups. In the finite-MOM normalization these
+were
+\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
+ P_{10}&=718.7129769223133, &\widehat\sigma_P&=0.0414043282783,\\
+ S_{26}&=-188.2890632768604, &\widehat\sigma_{26}&=0.1002179257784,\\
+ S_{40}&=-2.129275292198507, &\widehat\sigma_{40}&=0.0843482336693.
+\end{aligned}\label{meth:retained}\end{equation}
+These are not the separately MS-converted partial sectors printed in
+older appendices. Both fresh campaigns now integrate the 66
+nonprimitive kernels $S_{26}+S_{40}$, while retaining only the ten
+primitive kernels $P_{10}$. The previous production omits all 76,
+and its four-point diagnostics for them are never included.
+
+The four campaign jet means have fixed weights $1/4$. Let
+$S_{66}=S_{26}+S_{40}$. The combined value is
+\begin{equation}\boxed{
+ \widehat C_6(2)=
+ \mathcal F_6\left(\frac{\boldsymbol\mu^{\rm old,A}
+ +\boldsymbol\mu^{\rm old,B}+\boldsymbol\mu^{\rm new,A}
+ +\boldsymbol\mu^{\rm new,B}}4\right)
+ +P_{10}+\frac12 S_{66}.
+}\label{meth:combined}
+\end{equation}
+Here the first two sixth-loop vectors omit all 76 kernels, while the
+last two omit only ten. Lower-loop vectors are complete. Thus every
+required contribution has total expectation weight one. In particular,
+\begin{equation}
+ \widehat\sigma_{\rm common}^2
+ =\widehat\sigma_P^2+
+ \frac14(\widehat\sigma_{26}^2+\widehat\sigma_{40}^2).
+ \label{meth:common}
+\end{equation}
+No common input is averaged away as though independent. The fresh-only
+estimate instead adds $P_{10}$ once, with variance $\widehat\sigma_P^2$.
+At $d=3$ there is no separately retained numerical kernel offset.
+
+For each campaign and loop, let $\widehat\Sigma_{L,t}$ be the covariance
+of its mean estimated from independent randomized replicates. With
+$J_L=\partial\mathcal F_6/\partial\boldsymbol\mu_L$, the propagated
+sampling variance is
+\begin{equation}
+ \widehat{\operatorname{Var}}(\widehat C_6)
+ =\sum_{L,t}w_t^2J_L\widehat\Sigma_{L,t}J_L^{\mathsf T}
+ +\widehat\sigma_{\rm common}^2.
+ \label{meth:covariance}
+\end{equation}
+All coupling/mass and Taylor-order covariance is retained. The error
+budget, in units of $C_6$, is
+\begin{center}\begin{tabular}{@{}crrrrrr@{}}\toprule
+$d$ & $L=3$ & $L=4$ & $L=5$ & $L=6$ & Common & Total\\\midrule
+2 & 0.005635 & 0.037539 & 0.080576 & 0.096763 & 0.077485 & 0.152644 \\
+3 & 0.001145 & 0.005894 & 0.012458 & 0.019839 & 0.000000 & 0.024184 \\
+\bottomrule\end{tabular}\end{center}
+The common term is zero in three dimensions. Contributions in this
+table combine in quadrature, not by addition of standard errors.
+
+\subsection{Checks, method selection, and what has not been established}
+The same uncalibrated samples give the lower-order discrepancy checks
+\begin{center}\begin{tabular}{@{}crrr@{}}\toprule
+$d$ & $C_3$ pull & $C_4$ pull & $C_5$ pull\\\midrule
+2 & +0.09 & +0.50 & +0.60 \\
+3 & +0.44 & +0.80 & -0.21 \\
+\bottomrule\end{tabular}\end{center}
+The reference special-function values are checks only; their numerical
+values are not refitted or replaced by the Monte Carlo estimates.
+A separate finite two-variable Lagrange expansion agrees with the
+fixed-point iteration to $7\times10^{-13}$. Bootstrap resampling of the
+independent replicate vectors, including the common inputs once with
+\eqref{meth:common}, gives sampling errors 0.14987068 and 0.02376720.
+The bootstrap is a diagnostic, not an interval certification.
+The nonlinear delete-one-replicate bias estimates have magnitude
+below $3.4\times10^{-7}$ in $C_6$ units.
+
+\paragraph{Best supported choice for these integrands.}
+The strongest tested practical combination is local finite forest
+subtraction, tropical importance sampling, graph-stratified randomized
+Sobol nets, pilot-frozen cost-aware allocation, and shared polynomial
+products with monitored high-precision fallback. A separately trained
+transport is only modestly useful at $d=2$ and is unnecessary at $d=3$
+in the present tests. The runtime and variance gains are different
+quantities and have not been conflated. The total precision gain also
+uses more production samples; it is not all attributed to a change of
+sampling algorithm.
+
+Rank-one shifted lattice rules with smooth inverse-CDF importance
+fits are an established alternative for multi-loop integrals, including
+CPU and CUDA implementations interfaced to pySecDec~\cite{MethodLattice}.
+That implementation was researched but not benchmarked here, so it is
+not declared better or worse than the present Sobol method.
+Likewise, global tropical integration across primitive graphs has
+recently achieved much higher primitive loop orders~\cite{MethodGlobal};
+those positive primitive examples do not automatically establish
+performance for the signed, regulator-dependent renormalized forest
+combinations in this calculation.
+
+Conditional preintegration is a promising next structural improvement:
+for an unbiased integrand $Y$, exact integration of a subset of variables
+satisfies
+\begin{equation}
+ \operatorname{Var}(\mathbb E[Y\mid X])\leq\operatorname{Var}(Y).
+ \label{meth:rao}
+\end{equation}
+It is useful computationally only when its cost and any deterministic
+quadrature error are controlled. We have not replaced an integral by
+an unchecked low-order quadrature or assumed a favorable variance
+reduction. The unsuccessful first-edge test above illustrates why
+conditionalization must be benchmarked at equal cost.
+
+This appendix does not bound a shared error in graph generation,
+renormalization, or analytic continuation. It does not rerun the
+previous analytic power-sector compensation. Faster arithmetic and
+smaller sampling errors are not independent field-theoretic validation
+or rigorous error enclosures.
+
+\subsection{Reproduction and preservation}
+The delivered archive contains all fresh randomized replicate vectors,
+per-graph counts, the frozen transport tables, digital direction
+matrices, source hashes, method pilots, and the minimal previous
+production inputs needed for the fixed-weight combination. Reassembling
+requires no new integrations:
+\begin{lstlisting}
+python code/analyze.py
+python code/summarize_methods.py
+python code/extra_checks.py
+\end{lstlisting}
+The original displays remain unchanged and in their original order.
+Historical values are retained as history. The updated abstract and
+\eqref{meth:reported} identify the current precision. Raw means,
+standard errors, and the empirical reporting convention are stored
+separately so that a future precision change cannot silently reinterpret
+an old uncertainty.
